From: Ze contributors
Subject: [PATCH] ipsec: judge unprotected inbound traffic against the SPD

Type: fix

ipsec4-input-feature and ipsec6-input-feature look a packet up in the
interface's SPD only when it is ESP, AH or UDP to port 500 or 4500. Every
other packet counts as "IPSec policy not matched" and keeps the feature arc's
next node, whatever entry its selectors match: cleartext that matches an
inbound DISCARD entry, or the selectors of an inbound PROTECT entry, is
forwarded, and an inbound BYPASS entry is never consulted.

RFC 4301 Section 5.2 step 2: "Traffic not addressed to this device, or
addressed to this device and not AH or ESP, is directed to SPD-I lookup."
RFC 4301 Section 5.2 step 3b: "If there is a match and the packet is to be
discarded or bypassed, do so."

The input nodes cannot simply look every packet up: in policy mode the
packet esp4/esp6/ah4/ah6-decrypt produce goes back to ip4/ip6-input on the
interface the protected packet arrived on, with its RX interface unchanged,
and meets the same node again, where its selectors match the PROTECT entry
that decrypted it. The decrypt nodes therefore mark that packet with a new
buffer flag, VNET_BUFFER_F_IPSEC_DECRYPTED (the first free vnet bit, 19).
The input nodes read and clear the flag on every packet, so it reaches no
later lookup, and pass a marked packet.

An unmarked packet that is not ESP, AH or IKE is matched against the BYPASS
and DISCARD entries and the selectors of the PROTECT entries, protocol and
ports included, and the highest priority match decides: BYPASS passes it,
DISCARD and PROTECT drop it. On equal priority DISCARD wins over PROTECT and
PROTECT over BYPASS. A packet that matches nothing passes, as before; an SPD
that wants the RFC's final discard carries it as an entry. A packet whose
ports cannot be read (a non-initial IPv4 fragment, a transport header outside
the first buffer) matches only entries whose port ranges are whole.

New error counters: "IPSec cleartext matched protect" (dropped) and "IPSec
decrypted packet" (passed on its mark). A cleartext DISCARD or BYPASS match
counts under the existing "IPSec policy discard" and "IPSec policy bypass"
and in the entry's own packet counter.

Not covered: an SPD in fast-path mode (ipv4/ipv6-inbound-spd-fast-path) keeps
its entries out of spd->policies, so this lookup finds none and such an SPD
passes unprotected traffic as before. IPv6 extension headers are not walked.
Tunnel-protect (route-mode) SAs are not marked: their packets arrive on the
tunnel interface.

